1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,650
What if every customer, every team, and every AI agent could have

2
00:00:04,650 --> 00:00:08,545
a private database, without running a separate backend for each one?

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,300
This is Mira Base, running locally.

4
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:19,100
Create a collection, define typed fields, and save a real record in

5
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:24,900
the dashboard. Collections, authentication, access rules, files, realtime, and trusted hooks build

6
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:26,350
on Pocket Base.

7
00:00:27,150 --> 00:00:30,800
One running server. Four separate database files.

8
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:33,999
Alice gets a private user database.

9
00:00:35,150 --> 00:00:37,871
Bob gets a different user database.

10
00:00:39,150 --> 00:00:42,793
Northstar shares a tenant database across its team.

11
00:00:43,150 --> 00:00:45,993
The research agent has its own database.

12
00:00:47,150 --> 00:00:49,000
Bob tries Alice’s records.

13
00:00:49,350 --> 00:00:53,300
Denied. His own identity does not authorize her database.

14
00:00:53,650 --> 00:00:56,593
An operator adds an explicit read grant.

15
00:00:58,650 --> 00:01:01,910
Now Bob reads the same records successfully.

16
00:01:03,650 --> 00:01:08,071
Revoke the grant. His next request is denied again.

17
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:14,869
Alice can search her own workspace with full text, vector similarity, hybrid

18
00:01:14,869 --> 00:01:20,337
ranking, or fuzzy matching. The results shown here come from Turso through

19
00:01:20,337 --> 00:01:25,350
Mira Base. Application developers get these search APIs alongside their backend.

20
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:30,875
Files, realtime record events, and live query results connect the frontend to

21
00:01:30,875 --> 00:01:35,350
changes. Committed records also have a durable journal and record synchronization APIs.

22
00:01:36,150 --> 00:01:40,249
An operator creates a persistent workspace for the research agent.

23
00:01:40,950 --> 00:01:43,527
Write a note into its working file.

24
00:01:44,950 --> 00:01:47,871
Save a snapshot of the original bytes.

25
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:51,882
Replace the note with different contents.

26
00:01:53,950 --> 00:01:57,800
Restore the snapshot. The original note returns.

27
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:03,033
Operators can inspect jobs and diagnostics, export data, and create backups.

28
00:02:03,033 --> 00:02:08,087
Pocket Base adoption has a real dashboard workflow: analyze, preview, stage, validate,

29
00:02:08,087 --> 00:02:09,350
then cut over.

30
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:15,361
We used Muse Spark one point three to build Mira Base and

31
00:02:15,361 --> 00:02:17,428
a separate Quint-based verification harness.

32
00:02:17,428 --> 00:02:21,562
It connects claims, executable models, adversarial checks, and reproducible evidence.

33
00:02:21,562 --> 00:02:23,629
Bounds and failures stay visible.

34
00:02:23,629 --> 00:02:27,350
This is not a blanket proof of the backend.

35
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:33,987
Next we close policy and platform qualification gaps, complete offline clients and

36
00:02:33,987 --> 00:02:37,711
native sync, and integrate full Agent F S.

37
00:02:37,711 --> 00:02:40,039
We will investigate Wasm packaging.

38
00:02:40,039 --> 00:02:45,626
Our designed managed platform adds placement, replication, routing, and failover underneath the

39
00:02:45,626 --> 00:02:49,350
same backend model. Those cloud capabilities are planned.

40
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:52,531
Start on your own server.

41
00:02:52,531 --> 00:02:55,940
Give users, tenants, and agents their own data.

42
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:58,071
Mira Base: build your frontend.

43
00:02:58,071 --> 00:02:59,350
Own your backend.
